sum='Mahendra, a junior supervisor, moves from site to site (he works for a firm that offers supervisors at construction sites). His cook Iswaran follows him everywhere; he is an asset – he can produce vegetables and food in the most out-of-the-way places, cook delicious meals and keep Mahendra entertained with stories every night. Iswaran is influenced by the Tamil thrillers he reads, so he builds suspense and surprise in every story – even about an uprooted tree on the highway (“the road was deserted and I was all alone… suddenly I saw something like an enormous bushy beast lying sprawled across the road”). He tells how he controlled a mad tusker that escaped from the timber yard by whacking its third toenail with a small stick – hardly plausible. He more than makes up for the absence of a TV. When Mahendra’s factory site was once a burial ground, Iswaran tells him about a female ghost holding a foetus, who appears on full-moon nights. Mahendra calls ghosts a figment of the imagination, but on a full-moon night he hears a moaning sound and sees a dark cloudy form clutching a bundle at the window; he is terrified. The next morning he decides to leave the place, which he believes is haunted.',
